#### Comparison to other measurements

\(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) measurements

The STAR Collaboration at RHIC results [20] are following: for \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) in p+p at \(\sqrt{s}\) = 200 GeV = 575 \(\pm\) 11 (tower-track) MeV/c, 598 \(\pm\) 28 (track-track) MeV/c, and in d+Au at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV = 513 \(\pm\) 46 (tower-track) MeV/c, 565 \(\pm\) 44 (track-track) MeV/c.

The PHENIX Collaboration at RHIC measured [13]\(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) from di-hadron correlations in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}\) = 200 GeV. Their result is \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) = 585 \(\pm\) 6 (stat) \(\pm\) 15 (sys) MeV/c.

Measurements of \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) at the PHENIX and at the STAR experiment from di-hadron correlations (Figure 2.13, 2.14) shows that \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) doesn't significantly change with jet energy. It also shows up that p+p results are comparable with d+Au \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) values.

**Measurements of \(\left\langle\left|k_{Ty}\right|\right\rangle\left\langle z_{trig}\right\rangle\)**

The PHENIX Collaboration at RHIC measured [14] in case of p+p data at \(\sqrt{s}\) = 200 GeV, \(\left\langle\left|k_{Ty}\right|\right\rangle\left\langle z_{trig}\right\rangle\) = 0.91 \(\pm\) 0.04 GeV/c and for d+Au at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV, \(\left\langle\left|k_{Ty}\right|\right\rangle\left\langle z_{trig}\right\rangle\) = 1.16 \(\pm\) 0.06 GeV/c.

The STAR and the PHENIX Collaboration measured \(\sqrt{\left\langle k_{t}^{s^{2}}\right\rangle}\) (Figure 2.15) from di-hadron correlations shows similar conclusions as already above mentioned \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\).

## Chapter 5 Summary and Conclusion

The main goal of the diploma thesis was a study of azimuthal correlations of hadrons with high transverse momentum in nucleus-nucleus collisions, and their practical application on data from d+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV measured by the STAR collaboration at RHIC in 2008.

In this work we have used the neutral clusters from the BEMC and charged tracks from the TPC as trigger particles for studying the correlations with regard to the associated charged particles. From azimuthal correlation function we extracted parameters like widths, yields of near- and away-side peaks, \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) and \(\left\langle\left|k_{Ty}\right|\right\rangle\) characterizing the jet properties.

During work on this thesis we analysed d+Au collisions in detail. Values of \(j_{T}\) and \(k_{T}\) were obtained using the information from the TPC and BEMC for various jet energies. These values of \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) are about 10% higher than other measurements at RHIC and in case of \(\left\langle\left|k_{Ty}\right|\right\rangle\left\langle z_{trig}\right\rangle\) this difference is about 10%. Within the errors, the obtained results of \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{2}\right\rangle}\) and \(\left\langle\left|k_{Ty}\right|\right\rangle\left\langle z_{trig}\right\rangle\) are in a good agreement with the published results.

